WEBVTT

00:00.500 --> 00:02.780
What we will talk
about in this video

00:02.780 --> 00:04.910
is the product
rule, which is one

00:04.910 --> 00:08.960
of the fundamental ways
of evaluating derivatives.

00:08.960 --> 00:10.570
And we won't prove
it in this video,

00:10.570 --> 00:12.700
but we will learn
how to apply it.

00:12.700 --> 00:15.860
And all it tells us is that
if we have a function that

00:15.860 --> 00:19.250
can be expressed as a product
of two functions-- so let's

00:19.250 --> 00:22.960
say it can be expressed as
f of x times g of x-- and we

00:22.960 --> 00:27.610
want to take the derivative
of this function,

00:27.610 --> 00:30.270
that it's going to be equal
to the derivative of one

00:30.270 --> 00:32.350
of these functions,
f prime of x--

00:32.350 --> 00:34.530
let's say the derivative
of the first one times

00:34.530 --> 00:40.400
the second function
plus the first function,

00:40.400 --> 00:42.900
not taking its derivative,
times the derivative

00:42.900 --> 00:44.770
of the second function.

00:44.770 --> 00:46.120
So here we have two terms.

00:46.120 --> 00:48.560
In each term, we took
the derivative of one

00:48.560 --> 00:50.220
of the functions
and not the other,

00:50.220 --> 00:52.470
and we multiplied the
derivative of the first function

00:52.470 --> 00:54.800
times the second
function plus just

00:54.800 --> 00:56.520
the first function
times the derivative

00:56.520 --> 00:58.320
of the second function.

00:58.320 --> 01:00.890
Now let's see if we can actually
apply this to actually find

01:00.890 --> 01:02.790
the derivative of something.

01:02.790 --> 01:05.700
So let's say we are dealing
with-- I don't know--

01:05.700 --> 01:12.227
let's say we're dealing with
x squared times cosine of x.

01:12.227 --> 01:13.560
Or let's say-- well, yeah, sure.

01:13.560 --> 01:15.320
Let's do x squared
times sine of x.

01:15.320 --> 01:16.960
Could have done it either way.

01:16.960 --> 01:20.340
And we are curious about
taking the derivative of this.

01:20.340 --> 01:23.910
We are curious about
what its derivative is.

01:23.910 --> 01:26.710
Well, we might
immediately recognize

01:26.710 --> 01:28.430
that this is the
product of-- this

01:28.430 --> 01:30.880
can be expressed as a
product of two functions.

01:30.880 --> 01:35.320
We could set f of x
is equal to x squared,

01:35.320 --> 01:37.840
so that is f of x
right over there.

01:37.840 --> 01:44.175
And we could set g of x
to be equal to sine of x.

01:44.175 --> 01:45.050
And there we have it.

01:45.050 --> 01:48.054
We have our f of x times g of x.

01:48.054 --> 01:50.470
And we could think about what
these individual derivatives

01:50.470 --> 01:51.140
are.

01:51.140 --> 01:54.520
The derivative of f of x is
just going to be equal to 2x

01:54.520 --> 01:59.270
by the power rule, and
the derivative of g of x

01:59.270 --> 02:00.920
is just the derivative
of sine of x,

02:00.920 --> 02:02.545
and we covered this
when we just talked

02:02.545 --> 02:03.600
about common derivatives.

02:03.600 --> 02:06.740
Derivative of sine
of x is cosine of x.

02:06.740 --> 02:09.250
And so now we're ready to
apply the product rule.

02:09.250 --> 02:13.260
This is going to be equal to
f prime of x times g of x.

02:13.260 --> 02:15.390
So f prime of x--
the derivative of f

02:15.390 --> 02:24.260
is 2x times g of x, which
is sine of x plus just

02:24.260 --> 02:28.360
our function f,
which is x squared

02:28.360 --> 02:31.810
times the derivative of
g, times cosine of x.

02:35.170 --> 02:35.860
And we're done.

02:35.860 --> 02:39.430
We just applied
the product rule.
